# @io/git — the git protocol adapter at the @io altitude. Sibling
# species to @io/oci (commit 2801478, this session). The boundary
# species that gives the substrate addressable, transportable, content-
# addressed VERSIONED transport for per-peer-home-repo composition.
#
# Recognition #98 candidate territory's FOURTH witness (Mara/Alex,
# 2026-06-24): content-addressing IS a substrate-cross-altitude
# composition primitive. mirror's oid, Nix's derivation hash, OCI's
# digest, and now git's blob/tree/commit hash compose into a deeper
# closed loop at the content-addressing altitude. Same structural
# pattern; FOUR independent scoping disciplines; SHA1 vs SHA256
# function-altitude split documented in §5.
#
# === What @io/git IS ===
#
# Substrate-decl adapter naming the git protocol (git plumbing per
# Torvalds 2005, Git 2.x stable as of 2026-06-24) at the @io boundary.
# Typed surface for clone, fetch, read_object, resolve_ref,
# commit_object against git repositories — local or remote, libgit2 /
# gitoxide / shell-bridged at the realisation boundary.
#
# Per @io family-root (T21, 2026-06-08): @io is the substrate's only
# legitimate non-mirror surface; species parameterizes the boundary;
# the call body is opaque by construction.
#
# === Why now ===
#
# Alex's 2026-06-24 directive: build @spectral/garden/git abstraction
# layer using @git; each peer becomes their home repo (per recognition
# #84 @pack multi-repo agent runtime). @io/git is the sibling of
# @io/oci (commit 2801478) at the @io adapter altitude. Same content-
# addressing family pattern; same Splinter/Narcissus discipline; same
# composed-bilateral lift.
#
# Phase A of the LOCAL-PACK loop substrate-decls THIS adapter. Phase B
# forward-promised: shards/spectral/garden/git.mirror composes per-
# peer-home-repo identity. Phase C forward-promised: shards/mirror/
# agent.mirror's agent_spawn consumes peer git_ref for identity-
# substrate loading.
#
# === The ouroboros depth +1 ===
#
# Mara's @io/oci §6 named the depth: three SHA256-based content-
# addressing schemes (oid + derivation hash + OCI digest). @io/git
# DEEPENS the recognition by one scope while OPENING the function-
# altitude partition:
#
# - Nix derivation hash    SHA256, input closure / ca outputs
# - mirror oid             SHA256, substrate content bytes
# - OCI digest             SHA256, manifest + layer descriptors
# - git object hash        SHA1 (legacy) / SHA256 (transitioning),
#                          blob / tree / commit / tag content
#
# Same STRUCTURAL pattern; DIFFERENT hash function at the git scope;
# DIFFERENT scoping discipline (versioned object graph vs flat bytes
# vs build-input-closure vs manifest-layer-graph). The loop CLOSES at
# the content-addressing structural altitude; OPENS at every scoping
# AND function-altitude.
#
# === Recognition ancestry ===
#
# - #56 (substrate self-application): @io/git substrate-decls a
#   boundary the substrate ITSELF crosses (per-peer-home-repo
#   composition; each Pack agent IS their own git repo).
# - #58 (substrate IS Connes (A, H, D)): the adapter is part of A
#   operating on H with D discharging at the @io boundary against
#   external content-addressed space (git object database).
# - #84 (@pack multi-repo agent runtime): the load-bearing structural
#   ancestor; @io/git makes per-peer-home-repo composition operational.
# - #98 candidate (content-addressing cross-altitude composition):
#   @io/git is the FOURTH witness; promotion still gated on Pack
#   pressure + empirical discharge per @io/oci §6.
#
# === The Splinter/Narcissus pole at git-protocol altitude ===
#
# Splinter-pole (honest): clone returns actual git object graph; fetch
# verifies SHA1/SHA256 hashes on received objects; commit_object
# computes the hash byte-deterministically per git's content-hashing
# rules; hash_to_oid acknowledges the function-altitude partition.
# K_n peer-to-peer — each Pack peer's home repo IS a peer in the
# substrate network, content-addressed via the object graph.
#
# Narcissus-pole (deceptive): clone accepts objects without hash
# verification; commit_object returns a hash the realisation didn't
# compute; hash_to_oid claims SHA1↔SHA256 identity without naming
# the partition. K_{1,n-1} hub-controlled — central server reframes
# object graphs; per-peer composition collapses to single-source-of-
# truth (Narcissus failure mode #84 explicitly rejects).
#
# Alignment-as-boundary-mathematics (#57) IS what distinguishes the
# poles at substrate altitude. The composed bilateral `git_well_formed`
# lifts the substrate-architectural check.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
#   git plumbing (Torvalds 2005)   substrate ancestor; content-
#                                  addressed object database; SHA1
#                                  default; SHA256 transition Git 2.29+.
#   Mercurial (Mackall 2005)       contemporaneous DVCS; sibling.
#   Pijul (Becker 2015)            patch-theoretic DVCS alternative.
#   libgit2 (2008+)                C-library realisation alternative.
#   gitoxide (Rust 2020+)          pure-Rust realisation alternative.
#   OCI (OpenContainers 2024)      sibling adapter (commit 2801478).
#   IPFS (Benet 2014)              content-addressed transport at
#                                  different scope.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/recognitions/recognition-98-content-
#               addressing-cross-altitude-composition.md (forward-
#               promised; Pack-surfacing pending; this shard adds
#               fourth witness);
#               docs/specs/spectral-garden-git-v0.1.md (forward-
#               promised Phase B wire spec for per-peer-home-repo
#               composition).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-alignment-as-boundary-mathematics]],
#                      [[feedback-loss-from-epistemologic-properties]],
#                      [[feedback-no-bare-types]],
#                      [[feedback-composition-claims-need-empirical-test]].
# Recognition ancestry: #56, #58, #84, #98 candidate (fourth witness).
# Related shards: io.mirror, io/stagefreight.mirror, io/oci.mirror
#                 (sibling adapter, commit 2801478), io/cargo.mirror,
#                 labeled.mirror, spectral/garden/git.mirror
#                 (FORWARD-PROMISED Phase B), mirror/agent.mirror
#                 (FORWARD-PROMISED Phase C).
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/io/git.mirror`
# declares `@io/git` per `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_namespace`.

source @arxiv/version-control/torvalds-2005
source @arxiv/version-control/mackall-2005-mercurial
source @arxiv/distributed/benet-2014

prism @io/git {
  focus git
  project git
  split git
  shift git
  settle git
}

# ============================================================
# §2 The carriers
# ============================================================

# === The git_hash carrier ===
#
# Substrate-typed reference for a git object content-address. Format
# per Torvalds 2005: legacy SHA1 default (40-hex) or SHA256 transitional
# (64-hex; Git 2.29+). The function-altitude partition is explicit at
# this carrier — both hash functions are admissible at substrate-decl
# altitude; species shards specialize where needed.
#
# Per recognition #98 candidate: git_hash is structurally adjacent to
# mirror oid, OCI digest, and Nix derivation hash — SAME content-
# addressing structural pattern, DIFFERENT hash function. `hash_to_oid`
# makes the function-altitude crossing typed.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the underlying ref.
type git_hash = ref

# === The git_ref carrier ===
#
# Substrate-typed reference for a git ref (branch / tag / commit /
# HEAD / detached). Per git plumbing: `refs/heads/<branch>`,
# `refs/tags/<tag>`, `HEAD`, or raw SHA. Ref-validity discharges via
# `ref_well_formed` bilateral below.
#
# Per @pack multi-repo (#84): each Pack peer's home repo has a
# canonical HEAD naming their identity-substrate at a moment;
# git_ref IS the substrate-vocabulary for that moment-naming.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the underlying ref.
type git_ref = ref

# === The git_repository carrier ===
#
# Substrate-typed reference for a git repository — local path or
# remote URL (ssh://, https://, git://, file://). Repository-kind
# discrimination discharges at species-altitude refinement.
#
# Per Alex 2026-06-24: each peer becomes their home repo. The
# git_repository carrier IS the substrate-vocabulary for peer-as-repo
# identity.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the underlying ref.
type git_repository = ref

# === The git_object carrier ===
#
# Substrate-typed reference for a git object (blob / tree / commit /
# tag). Every object addressable by its content hash (git_hash).
# Object-kind discrimination is structural in git's object database;
# species-altitude carriers can refine to typed blob / tree / commit /
# tag if useful.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the underlying ref.
type git_object = ref

# === The git_ref_metadata carrier ===
#
# Substrate-typed reference for a git ref's symbolic information:
# ref-name, ref-kind, optional upstream tracking, optional annotated-
# tag payload. Companion to git_object in the labeled<> pairing below.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the underlying ref.
type git_ref_metadata = ref

# === The git_artifact carrier ===
#
# Per @labeled (recognition #93 H4 RESOLVED 2026-06-23): the
# deliverable git artifact is a labeled pair (git_object,
# git_ref_metadata). The labeled<> functor adds the ref-context
# dimension without losing the underlying typed object reference.
#
# Composes structurally with @io/oci's oci_artifact = labeled(ref,
# oci_manifest) — same parametric primitive, different (value, label)
# pair. The substrate's functor library is load-bearing here.
#
# At @pack multi-repo altitude: git_artifact carries a peer's
# identity-substrate (the git_object) labeled with the moment it was
# captured (the git_ref_metadata).
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the labeled pair.
type git_artifact = labeled(git_object, git_ref_metadata)

# ============================================================
# §3 The actions
# ============================================================

# === clone action ===
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING bootstrap action. Clones a remote git repository
# to a local target path; returns the local git_repository reference
# at the realisation boundary.
#
# requires git_reachable(remote, p): the remote MUST be reachable
# with required credentials before clone is admissible. Without this
# gate, clone would silently fail or partial-state (the Narcissus-
# pole network failure mode).
#
# Returns imperfect<git_repository, ref, ref> per the @io family-root
# convention:
# - success(git_repository) — clone completed; local repo ready
# - partial(git_repository, transparency) — clone completed with
#   recoverable warnings (e.g., shallow clone, missing submodules)
# - failure(error, transparency) — clone failed (network, auth,
#   ref-not-found, protocol error)
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary (libgit2 / gitoxide /
# shell-out to git binary at @io edge).
clone(remote: git_repository, target: ref, p: perturbation)
  -> imperfect(git_repository, ref, ref)
requires git_reachable(remote, p)
{ \ }

# === fetch action ===
#
# Fetch a git_ref from an opened repository; returns the typed
# git_object the ref resolves to.
#
# requires git_repository_open(repo, p): the repository must be
# readable at the @io boundary before fetch is admissible.
#
# Returns imperfect<git_object, ref, ref> per @io convention.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
fetch(repo: git_repository, ref: git_ref, p: perturbation)
  -> imperfect(git_object, ref, ref)
requires git_repository_open(repo, p)
{ \ }

# === read_object action ===
#
# Read a git_object from a repository by its content-hash. The
# substrate-vocabulary primitive for content-addressed retrieval at
# the git scope.
#
# requires hash_well_formed(hash, p): the hash must be syntactically
# valid (SHA1 hex or SHA256 hex per the function-altitude partition)
# before read is admissible.
#
# Returns imperfect<git_object, ref, ref>. The realisation MUST verify
# the received object hashes to the requested git_hash (per the
# Splinter-pole discipline; analogous to OCI's digest_matches_content).
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
read_object(repo: git_repository, hash: git_hash, p: perturbation)
  -> imperfect(git_object, ref, ref)
requires hash_well_formed(hash, p)
{ \ }

# === resolve_ref action ===
#
# Resolve a git_ref (symbolic; branch / tag / HEAD) to its concrete
# git_hash. The bridge between symbolic naming and content-addressing
# at the git scope.
#
# Returns imperfect<git_hash, ref, ref>. The realisation discharges
# the ref-resolution at the @io boundary.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
resolve_ref(repo: git_repository, ref: git_ref, p: perturbation)
  -> imperfect(git_hash, ref, ref)
{ \ }

# === commit_object action ===
#
# Create a new commit object in a repository given a tree hash, parent
# commit hashes, and a message. Returns the byte-deterministically
# computed git_hash of the new commit (per git plumbing's content-
# hashing rules: hash of `commit <size>\0<header>\n<message>`).
#
# Returns imperfect<git_hash, ref, ref>. The realisation MUST compute
# the hash per git's specification AND have the new commit retrievable
# via read_object before returning success (Splinter-pole discipline).
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
commit_object(repo: git_repository, tree: git_hash,
              parents: ref, message: ref, p: perturbation)
  -> imperfect(git_hash, ref, ref)
{ \ }

# === hash_to_oid action ===
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING BRIDGE ACTION grounding recognition #98 candidate
# FOURTH-witness contribution. Lifts a git_hash (SHA1 default or
# SHA256 transitional) into mirror's oid space (Splinter; SHA256 of
# substrate content bytes).
#
# CRITICAL function-altitude partition: when git repo is SHA1-mode
# (default; Git 2.x), git_hash is SHA1(B) while mirror oid is
# SHA256(B') for substrate bytes B'. The lift is NOT byte-identity;
# it is a SUBSTRATE-typed bridge that re-hashes the underlying git
# object bytes under SHA256 to produce the oid.
#
# When git repo is SHA256-mode (Git 2.29+ transitional), git_hash is
# SHA256(B); the lift CAN be byte-identity at the function altitude
# but remains a TYPED OPERATION at the substrate altitude — scoping
# discipline differs (git object scope vs substrate-bytes scope).
#
# The substrate does NOT pretend SHA1 and SHA256 are the same
# function; the typed lift forces the realisation to acknowledge
# the scope and function-altitude crossings. Per recognition #98
# candidate FOURTH-witness: this action makes the partition LEGIBLE
# at substrate altitude. SHA1 has known collision attacks (SHAttered,
# 2017); species shards CAN refuse SHA1-mode lifts where security
# matters.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
hash_to_oid(h: git_hash, p: perturbation) -> ref { \ }

# ============================================================
# §4 The bilaterals
# ============================================================

# === git_reachable predicate (sub-bilateral) ===
#
# Bilateral verdict that a remote git_repository is reachable with the
# required credentials. Discharge invokes ls-remote or equivalent at
# the realisation boundary.
#
# Splinter-pole: remote reachable; auth accepted; refs listable.
# Narcissus-pole: network unreachable, auth rejected, endpoint not
# git-protocol-conformant.
git_reachable(remote: git_repository, p: perturbation) -> verdict { \ }

# === git_repository_open predicate (sub-bilateral) ===
#
# Bilateral verdict that a local git_repository is openable and
# structurally sound (`.git/` directory present, HEAD readable, object
# database accessible). Precondition for fetch / read_object /
# commit_object.
#
# Splinter-pole: repository opens cleanly; object database accessible.
# Narcissus-pole: `.git/` missing or corrupt; HEAD unreadable; object
# database inaccessible.
git_repository_open(repo: git_repository, p: perturbation) -> verdict { \ }

# === hash_well_formed predicate (sub-bilateral) ===
#
# Bilateral verdict that a git_hash is syntactically valid: SHA1 hex
# (40 chars, [0-9a-f]) OR SHA256 hex (64 chars, [0-9a-f]) per git's
# function-altitude partition.
#
# Splinter-pole: hash matches one of the two admissible hex forms.
# Narcissus-pole: malformed hex; wrong length; mixed case where
# canonical lower required.
hash_well_formed(h: git_hash, p: perturbation) -> verdict { \ }

# === ref_well_formed predicate (sub-bilateral) ===
#
# Bilateral verdict that a git_ref follows git's ref-naming rules per
# git-check-ref-format (no `..`, no leading `/`, no trailing `.lock`,
# admissible character classes, etc.) OR is a raw SHA-shaped string
# resolvable as a commit hash.
#
# Splinter-pole: ref follows git's ref-naming discipline.
# Narcissus-pole: malformed ref-name; would be rejected by git itself.
ref_well_formed(r: git_ref, p: perturbation) -> verdict { \ }

# === git_well_formed composed bilateral ===
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING composed bilateral at @io/git altitude. Composes
# three sub-predicates:
#
#   1. git_repository_open(repo, p)
#   2. hash_well_formed(hash_of(artifact), p) — uses labeled<>
#      projection per Mara A3 surprise (labeled functor's value-
#      side accessor) to extract the git_object's hash from the
#      git_artifact pair
#   3. ref_well_formed(label_of(artifact), p) — uses labeled<>
#      projection (label-side accessor) to extract the
#      git_ref_metadata from the git_artifact pair
#
# This is the ~13th altitude lift of the composed-bilateral pattern
# that landed in earnest with @io/stagefreight.stagefreight_addressable
# and propagated through @io/oci.oci_well_formed (commit 2801478 this
# session as the ~12th). The pattern is substrate-pull-confident at
# this point; the count is Mara-counted-from-brief and verification is
# deferred to Pack altitude.
#
# Splinter-pole: all three sub-predicates discharge; artifact is
# well-formed across opening, content-hashing, and ref-naming.
# Narcissus-pole: any one fails; alignment-as-boundary-mathematics
# (#57) distinguishes which sub-claim broke.
git_well_formed(artifact: git_artifact, repo: git_repository,
                p: perturbation) -> verdict
requires git_repository_open(repo, p)
requires hash_well_formed(artifact, p)
requires ref_well_formed(artifact, p)
{ \ }

# ============================================================
# §5 The git ↔ content-addressing composition (ouroboros +1)
# ============================================================
#
# This section DOCUMENTS the FOURTH-witness contribution to recognition
# #98 candidate territory. The substrate-decl carries the SHAPE; the
# discharge is Pack-altitude empirical confirmation.
#
# === The structural claim ===
#
# FOUR content-addressing schemes at different scopes AND across a
# function-altitude partition:
#
# - Nix derivation hash    SHA256, identifies a BUILD (inputs / ca outputs)
# - mirror oid             SHA256, identifies SUBSTRATE content bytes
# - OCI digest             SHA256, identifies an ARTIFACT (manifest + layers)
# - git object hash        SHA1 default / SHA256 transitional, identifies
#                          a VERSIONED git object (blob/tree/commit/tag)
#
# Three of the four share SHA256 at the function altitude; the fourth
# (git default) is SHA1. STRUCTURAL pattern (content-addressing via
# cryptographic hash) shared across all four; FUNCTION splits 3:1;
# SCOPING splits 4:4. The loop CLOSES at the content-addressing
# structural altitude; OPENS at every scoping + hash-function altitude.
#
# === The ouroboros deepens BY ONE scope with @io/git ===
#
#   substrate-decl describes A (has oid)
#     → Nix derivation builds A (has hash)
#       → OCI manifest packages A (has digest)
#         → git commit records A's source provenance (has git_hash)
#           → registry / git remote stores A (addressable by hash)
#             → Nix pulls A back / git clones A's source
#               → substrate-decl consumes A (oid + git_hash recoverable)
#                 → describes B that depends on A → loop at altitude+1
#
# === Recognition #98 fourth-witness, function-altitude addition ===
#
# Mara's @io/oci §6 (commit 2801478) flagged content-addressing as
# substrate-cross-altitude composition primitive with THREE SHA256
# witnesses. @io/git adds the FOURTH (git object hash) AND forces the
# function-altitude partition (SHA1 vs SHA256) into the open.
#
# SHA1 has known collision attacks (SHAttered, 2017); Git 2.29+
# supports SHA256 repos; transition slow as of 2026-06-24 point-in-
# time. The substrate-decl ADMITS both; hash_to_oid makes the
# function-altitude crossing typed; consumers MUST NOT assume blanket
# identity across SHA1↔SHA256 or across scope.
#
# This shard DEEPENS the evidence but does NOT cross the promotion
# gate. Pack-altitude question pending: does the SHA1/SHA256 split
# STRENGTHEN the recognition (hash-function-independent pattern) or
# WEAKEN it (pattern dilutes to "cryptographic hashing")?

# ============================================================
# §6 Forward-promised consumers
# ============================================================
#
# - shards/spectral/garden/git.mirror — Phase B of LOCAL-PACK loop;
#   per-peer-home-repo composition; each Pack peer's home git repo
#   becomes a typed garden-instance carrier; cross-peer git-mediated
#   state-sharing becomes substrate-vocabulary.
# - shards/mirror/agent.mirror — Phase C; agent_spawn primitive
#   consumes peer git_ref for identity-substrate loading; peers boot
#   from their home repo's git_ref at spawn time.
# - shards/io/git/<protocol>.mirror — species refinement by protocol
#   (ssh / https / file / git://) where SEL auth shape differs.
# - StageFreight integration potentially: git-based artifact fetching
#   as alternative to OCI; freight_manifest could carry oci_reference
#   OR git_ref+git_hash pair as underlying address.
# - shards/io/git/forge/<forge>.mirror — forge-specific refinements
#   (GitHub / GitLab / Gitea / Codeberg / self-hosted) lifting PR,
#   issue, release surfaces to substrate vocabulary.

# ============================================================
# §7 Honest hedges
# ============================================================
#
# 1. Git spec version pinned point-in-time 2026-06-24 (Git 2.x stable;
#    SHA256 transition Git 2.29+ supported but slow). Realisation
#    must track git version and decline unsupported behaviors.
#
# 2. SHA1 vs SHA256 function-altitude split is NAMED in §5; hash_to_
#    oid discharges the partition. Substrate-decl does NOT pretend
#    SHA1 is secure for new content addressing; SHAttered (2017)
#    demonstrated practical collisions; species shards CAN refuse
#    SHA1-mode lifts where threat model requires.
#
# 3. No typed auth surface yet (SSH key / token / oauth). Auth at
#    SEL boundary per [[architecture-type-sel-io-au]]; species-
#    altitude refinement carries typed auth.
#
# 4. Implementation choice at realisation boundary: libgit2 vs
#    gitoxide vs shelling-out-to-git. Substrate-decl is implementation-
#    agnostic; impl discharges.
#
# 5. Recognition #98 fourth-witness claim adds evidence but doesn't
#    cross promotion gate (Pack pressure + empirical discharge still
#    required per @io/oci §6). The SHA1/SHA256 dimension is NEW;
#    impact on the candidate's case is Pack-altitude conversation.
#
# 6. Composed-bilateral count claim ("13th altitude lift") is
#    Mara-counted-from-brief; verification deferred to Pack altitude.
#
# 7. Per-peer-home-repo composition (#84) at Phase B will surface
#    its own questions: identity persistence across rebase / force-
#    push? merge semantics across peer histories? canonical-branch
#    policy? Forward-promised; not load-bearing for Phase A.
#
# 8. The "ouroboros depth +1" framing is Mara's structural extension
#    of @io/oci §6. Whether deepening STRENGTHENS or WEAKENS the
#    recognition is Pack-altitude question.

out @io/git
out git_hash
out git_ref
out git_repository
out git_object
out git_ref_metadata
out git_artifact
out clone
out fetch
out read_object
out resolve_ref
out commit_object
out hash_to_oid
out git_reachable
out git_repository_open
out hash_well_formed
out ref_well_formed
out git_well_formed
